  "explanation": {
    "event_selection": "Every event in the protocol is included only if required for ordered delivery to an identified zone with arbitration. All color object/zone visible/not visible/reached observations, claim/received-claim for arbitration, and all relevant search/approach/pickup/drop events are selected. No low-level or unrelated motion commands are present inline; search_object, search_zone, approach_object, and approach_zone are used as required and self-looped in all active states as the objective and runtime contract specify. No unrelated patrolling or repeated-goal functionality is synthesized.",
    "transition_design": "The automaton starts in search_object_unclaimed (searching for any unclaimed object). search_object self-loops here as allowed. On a color object visible, it branches to a color-specific claim attempt. Only after this, claim_<color> can be issued. If another robot already claims, received_claim_<color> immediately returns to object search. Successful claiming advances protocol to approach (allowing repeat approach_object, and visibility reacquisition/loss). Arrival at the object (with or without visible) enables pickup, after which it transitions to zone search (with self-looping search_zone), zone approach, and zone reached, before finally enabling drop_object in one step. All branches converge on 'delivered', the sole marked state. No transient or incomplete state is marked. Only legitimate protocol-compliant transitions are created.",
    "feedback_response": "The previous automaton failed semantic validation because search_object was not a self-loop in its search state. This is corrected by removing the unneeded 'start' state and replacing it with 'search_object_unclaimed' as the initial state, which always self-loops on search_object. All transitions requiring search_object as a self-loop in a search state now conform, and the protocol and structural requirements of the runtime contract are preserved."
